<section id="results">
  <h2>Results</h2>

  <h3>Dense 3D segmentations from sparse 2D annotations</h3>

  <p>We validated the 2D&rarr;3D method across six publicly available datasets: HARRIS-15<a href="#ref-1" class="cite"><sup>1</sup></a>, FIB-25<a href="#ref-2" class="cite"><sup>2</sup></a>, CREMI-A/B/C<a href="#ref-3" class="cite"><sup>3</sup></a>, and EPI<a href="#ref-4" class="cite"><sup>4</sup></a>. Each dataset contains two dense ground-truth volumes. We systematically varied annotation density from single objects to dense labels.</p>

  <p>Across all datasets, ten minutes of non-expert sparse annotation on a single section was sufficient to generate a dense 3D segmentation comparable in quality to one based on expert annotation requiring 1,000&times; more time.</p>

