  <h3>Generalization across imaging modalities and segmentation tasks</h3>

  <p>We tested the 2D&rarr;3D method on five additional publicly available datasets spanning distinct imaging modalities, biological structures, and segmentation tasks. Across all five, the pipeline produced visually coherent 3D segmentations from minimal annotation effort.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>LICONN</strong><a href="#ref-5" class="cite"><sup>5</sup></a>: Expansion-microscopy volumes of mouse somatosensory cortex (~10 &times; 10 &times; 20 nm effective voxel size). 10 minutes of SAM-assisted sparse annotation on a few sections for boundary segmentation.</li>
    <li><strong>PRISM</strong><a href="#ref-6" class="cite"><sup>6</sup></a>: 18-channel light microscopy of mouse hippocampus (35 &times; 35 &times; 80 nm). Ten sections of manual ground-truth annotation for boundary segmentation.</li>
    <li><strong>CREMI-C</strong><a href="#ref-3" class="cite"><sup>3</sup></a>: Serial-section TEM of <em>Drosophila</em> neuropil through an axon tract (4 &times; 4 &times; 40 nm). Two sections of manual annotation for synaptic cleft segmentation.</li>
    <li><strong>MitoEM-H</strong><a href="#ref-7" class="cite"><sup>7</sup></a>: Multi-beam SEM of human cortex (8 &times; 8 &times; 30 nm). Two sections of manual annotation for mitochondria instance segmentation.</li>
    <li><strong>Fluo-C2DL-Huh7</strong><a href="#ref-8" class="cite"><sup>8</sup></a>: Live cell laser confocal imaging of Huh7 hepatocarcinoma cells (0.65 &times; 0.65 &mu;m pixel size). Two frames of manual annotation for 2D+t cell segmentation.</li>
  </ul>

